UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{(i)} Since
\[ \int_n^\infty \Bigl(\frac xn - 1\Bigr) x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac{n^{-s}}{s(s-1)} \]
and $\sum_n \Lambda(n) n^{-\sigma} < \infty$ for $\sigma > 1$, we may integrate term by term:
\[ \int_1^\infty R_\chi(x)\, x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac{1}{s(s-1)} \sum_{n \ge 1} \Lambda(n)\chi(n)\, n^{-s} = -\frac{L''}{L}(s,\chi)\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)}. \]
The remaining terms come from $\int_1^\infty x \cdot x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac1{s-1}$ and $\int_1^\infty x\log x \cdot x^{-s-1}\,dx = \frac1{(s-1)^2}$.

\emph{(ii)} The right-hand sides in (i) are meromorphic on $\C$. At $s = 1$ the singular parts cancel: for $\chi \ne \mathbf 1$ because $-\frac{L''}{L}(1,\chi) = -b_\chi$, and for $\chi = \mathbf 1$ because $-\frac{\zeta''}{\zeta}(s) = \frac{1}{s-1} - \gamma + O(s-1)$ gives
\[ -\frac{\zeta''}{\zeta}(s)\,\frac{1}{s(s-1)} = \frac{1}{(s-1)^2} - \frac{1+\gamma}{s-1} + O(1). \]
At a zero $\rho$ of $L(s,\chi)$ with $\Re\rho > 0$ (so $\rho \ne 0, 1$), $-\frac{L''}{L}(s,\chi)$ has a simple pole with residue $-m_\chi(\rho)$, which gives the stated residue.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 14:10:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 106;
